course: biology
lesson: 168
title: The Arctic tundra: musk oxen, arctic foxes, snowy owls and the truth about lemmings
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The tundra is the flat, treeless land around the Arctic Ocean: frozen and dark for months in winter, and in summer bright day and night, boggy and buzzing with mosquitoes.
explain: Just under the surface lies permafrost, ground that stays frozen all year, so water cannot drain away, trees cannot root deeply, and plants grow low and close together like a carpet.
explain: Many tundra animals change clothes with the seasons, the way you swap a summer T-shirt for a winter coat: the arctic fox and the ptarmigan turn white for the snow and brown for the summer ground.
explain: Old stories, a 1958 nature film and a computer game made people think lemmings jump off cliffs together on purpose; they do not, and the film's lemmings were pushed off a cliff by the people making it.
fact: When wolves come, musk oxen stand in a ring with the calves in the middle and their horns pointing out.
fact: Musk ox underwool, called qiviut, is warmer than sheep's wool, and people collect it when it falls out in spring.
fact: The arctic fox has a brown or grey coat in summer and a thick white coat in winter, and it sometimes follows polar bears to eat their leftovers.
fact: Lemmings do not jump off cliffs on purpose to die; their numbers rise and fall in cycles, and when crowded they spread out and some drown crossing water.
fact: The cliff story spread from a 1958 nature film in which people pushed lemmings off a cliff for the camera.
fact: Snowy owls hunt lemmings and can hear them moving under the snow, and they hunt by day as well as at night because Arctic summer days never get dark.
fact: Caribou, called reindeer in Europe, make some of the longest land migrations of any animal, and their hooves are soft in summer for wet ground and hard and sharp in winter for digging snow.
fact: Arctic hares can hop on their back legs like a kangaroo when they run fast, and they sometimes gather in groups of hundreds.
fact: Arctic plants grow low and close together out of the wind, and some arctic flowers turn to follow the sun like little dishes that warm visiting insects.
fact: Permafrost is ground that stays frozen all year; in summer only a thin top layer thaws into mud, and scientists have found frozen woolly mammoths and ancient seeds in it.
fact: In summer the tundra has huge swarms of mosquitoes, so thick that caribou run to windy hills or snow patches to escape them.
fact: Ptarmigans are birds that turn white in winter and brown in summer, and they dig snow caves to sleep in because snow keeps the warmth in.
fact: The Arctic woolly bear caterpillar freezes solid each winter and thaws in summer, and it may take several years to become a moth because summers are so short.
exercise: What do musk oxen do when wolves come? | Stand in a ring, calves inside and horns out | Run off in all directions so the wolves get confused | Lie down and play dead | Hide the calves deep in burrows under the snow
exercise: What is qiviut? | Musk ox underwool, warmer than sheep's wool | A kind of soft white arctic flower | A snow cave dug by snowy owls for winter | A caribou hoof
exercise: What colour is the arctic fox in winter? | Thick white | Bright red | Dark brown | Black with spots
exercise: Why does an arctic fox sometimes follow a polar bear? | To eat its leftovers | To ride on its back | To keep it company | To find its den to live in
exercise: Do lemmings jump off cliffs on purpose to die? | No, that is a myth | Yes, every year in spring | Yes, when they are old | Only when a fox is chasing them
exercise: Where did the lemming cliff story spread from? | A 1958 film where people pushed lemmings off a cliff | A real study by many scientists over many years | Lemmings writing about themselves | Arctic hares telling stories
exercise: How does a snowy owl find a lemming under the snow? | It hears it moving | It smells it from the sky | It digs everywhere at random | It waits for it to jump off a cliff
exercise: Why do snowy owls hunt by day as well as at night? | Arctic summer days never get dark | They cannot see at night at all | Lemmings sleep only in daylight, so owls wait | They are scared of the dark
exercise: What are caribou called in Europe? | Reindeer | Elk | Moose | Musk oxen
exercise: How do caribou hooves change in winter? | They become hard and sharp for digging snow | They fall off every winter and grow back | They grow thick fur on the bottom | They turn into flippers
exercise: How does an arctic hare run fast? | It can hop on its back legs like a kangaroo | It rolls downhill like a ball to escape foxes | It slides on its belly | It flies short distances
exercise: Why do arctic plants grow low and close together? | To keep out of the cold wind | Because big animals keep sitting on them | Because they are lazy | To reach the sun better
exercise: How do some arctic flowers help insects? | They turn to the sun like little warm dishes | They give them tiny blankets of moss at night | They build them nests | They sting their enemies
exercise: What is permafrost? | Ground that stays frozen all year | Snow that falls only in summer | A kind of arctic fox | Ice that floats on the top of the sea
exercise: What have scientists found frozen in permafrost? | Woolly mammoths and ancient seeds | Living dinosaurs that were asleep | Penguins | Gold coins from the old Viking ships
exercise: How do caribou escape swarms of mosquitoes? | They run to windy hills or snow patches | They roll in cold mud only | They swat them away with their big antlers | They go into caves
exercise: Why does a ptarmigan dig a snow cave to sleep in? | Snow keeps the warmth in | To hide its eggs from foxes | To find worms | To cool down
exercise: Why may a woolly bear caterpillar take several years to become a moth? | Arctic summers are so short | It is lazy | It forgets how to make a cocoon | It is waiting for a mate
test: In the game Lemmings, little creatures march off cliffs unless you stop them. What is true of real lemmings? | They do not jump off cliffs on purpose; the idea is a myth | They march off cliffs every day | They need a player to save them | They float down from cliffs on tiny umbrellas, all together
test: «my teacher showed a video of lemmings jumping off a cliff so it's true» What is right? | In that old film the lemmings were pushed off by the film makers | Yes, filmed proof always means it is real | Yes, they do it every spring to make space for others | No, lemmings cannot climb cliffs
test: «4 musk oxen in the circle, how many horns pointing out» | 8 | 4 | 16 | 12
test: What would happen to an arctic fox that stayed brown in a snowy winter? | Its prey and enemies would spot it more easily | It would stay warmer | It would hide better on the dark rocks and moss | Nothing would change
test: «how do these owls hunt when it's daytime at midnight lol» What is right? | Snowy owls hunt in daylight too, because the summer sun never sets | They wear sunglasses | They do not hunt in summer at all, they live on fat | They wait for the winter dark
test: «caribou, reindeer, same thing or nah» What is right? | Yes, caribou are called reindeer in Europe | No, reindeer are moose | No, reindeer only live in stories and Christmas songs | No, caribou are a kind of goat
test: Builders dig into frozen ground in Siberia and find a hairy elephant-like animal. What is it most likely? | A woolly mammoth kept in the permafrost | A circus elephant with fur | A musk ox frozen in the ice of the sea | A walrus
test: «3 arctic hares hopping, no 5, how many long ears» | 10 | 6 | 5 | 20
test: «the ground's frozen but there's mud on top, wtf» What is right? | In summer only the top layer thaws, and the permafrost below stays frozen | The mud fell from the sky | The ground is frozen only on top, and warm and muddy far below | It is all frozen, there is no mud
test: Why might a caribou herd climb to a windy hill on a summer day? | To escape clouds of mosquitoes | To look for trees | To get away from the deep snow | To lay eggs
test: A caterpillar froze solid in winter and crawls away in summer. How is that possible? | The Arctic woolly bear caterpillar survives freezing and thawing | It was a plastic toy | It never really froze | It is a different caterpillar each time, hatched from new eggs
exam: Identify the principal defensive behaviour adopted by a herd of musk oxen upon the approach of wolves. | The formation of a ring with the calves enclosed and the horns outwards | Dispersal of the herd in all directions | The concealment of the calves within burrows excavated in the snow | Feigning death upon the ground
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the supposed mass suicide of lemmings. | It is a myth propagated in part by a film of 1958 in which lemmings were deliberately driven over a cliff | It is a well-attested phenomenon occurring every spring | It is undertaken by elderly lemmings exclusively | It occurs only when lemmings are pursued by foxes
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises permafrost? | Ground which remains frozen throughout the entire year | Snow which falls exclusively during the summer | Sea ice which forms in the winter months | A species of lichen of the tundra
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the snowy owl hunts during the hours of daylight. | The sun does not set during the Arctic summer | Its eyes are incapable of vision at night | Its prey is active exclusively in daylight during the summer months | It is fearful of darkness
